+// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+// M2_CAMERA_POINTER_CHANNEL_IMPL — the drag half of OnMouseMove, for a pointer that has
+// no position.
+// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+//
+// Delivered ONLY by CanvasInput and only while Pointer Lock is held; X11Input and EterInput
+// never call it and inherit the empty default. The argument is a VIRTUAL, UNCLAMPED pointer
+// position — lock-entry position plus every movement delta since — so it goes to Drag
+// unchanged and needs no arithmetic here.
+//
+// >>> AND THERE IS NO WARP, WHICH IS THE POINT RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. <<< OnMouseMove
+// above calls WarpCursorToClientPoint to pin the cursor; under lock there IS no cursor to
+// pin, and the browser arm of that function is a documented no-op
+// (PythonAppPlatformPosix.cpp:91-103). CanvasInput freezes the UI position for the duration
+// instead, which reaches the same end state the warp was built to produce.
+//
+// The UI is deliberately not run from here — no SetResolution, no RunMouseMove. The pointer
+// did not move as far as the UI is concerned, and telling it otherwise is exactly the
+// channel mixing this split exists to prevent.
+void CPythonApplication::OnCameraPointerMove(int virtualX, int virtualY)
+{
+	if (!EngineLib::Engine::Instance().GetScene().IsMapReady())
+		return;
+
+	auto& rkCam = EngineLib::Engine::Instance().GetCamera();
+
+	// Drag RE-ANCHORS to the position it is given (BgfxCamera.cpp:1221-1222), so consecutive
+	// calls yield exactly the movement between them. When no drag is in progress it
+	// re-anchors and answers false, which is the correct no-op.
+	int nDragX = 0, nDragY = 0;
+	rkCam.Drag(virtualX, virtualY, nDragX, nDragY);
+}
